/**
\addtogroup dense

The `laplace::dense` module provides host-only dense matrix decompositions, triangular system solves, symmetric and generalized eigendecompositions, and null-space bases in `<laplace/dense/dense.hpp>`. Unlike header-only `LAPLACE_HD` modules, these functions are compiled with Eigen into the static library `laplace_dense` (CMake target `Laplace::dense`) and run on one host thread per call over host-accessible Kokkos Views. Against 200-bit mpmath references on 17 cases, eigenvalues and singular values stay within 12.2 \f$\varepsilon\f$ for symmetric and rectangular problems, while Cholesky and triangular solve residuals stay within 0.62 \f$\varepsilon\f$.

## Decompositions and types

This section defines the seven operations of `laplace::dense` and its status and option types.

### Cholesky decomposition and triangular solve

For an \f$n \times n\f$ symmetric positive definite matrix \f$A\f$, \ref laplace::dense::cholesky_decomposition "cholesky_decomposition(A, L)" computes the lower-triangular factor \f$L\f$ satisfying \f$A = L L^\top\f$ (Golub and Van Loan, 2013), setting the strictly upper triangle of \f$L\f$ to zero.

For an \f$n \times n\f$ triangular matrix \f$T\f$ and an \f$n \times k\f$ matrix \f$B\f$ with \f$k\f$ right-hand sides, \ref laplace::dense::triangular_solve "triangular_solve(T, B, triangle, operation)" solves \f$\operatorname{op}(T) X = B\f$ in place on \f$B\f$. Here `triangle` selects the lower or upper triangle of \f$T\f$, and `operation` selects \f$\operatorname{op}(T) = T\f$ (`dense_operation::identity`) or \f$T^\top\f$ (`dense_operation::transpose`).

### Pivoted QR and singular value decompositions

For an \f$m \times n\f$ matrix \f$A\f$ with \f$p = \min(m, n)\f$, \ref laplace::dense::pivoted_qr_decomposition "pivoted_qr_decomposition(A, Q, R, perm, relative_tolerance)" computes \f$A P = Q R\f$ (Businger and Golub, 1965), where \f$Q\f$ has orthonormal columns, \f$R\f$ is upper triangular, and \f$P\f$ is the permutation in `perm` such that column \f$j\f$ of \f$Q R\f$ equals column \f$\operatorname{perm}[j]\f$ of \f$A\f$.

Under the same dimensions, \ref laplace::dense::svd_decomposition "svd_decomposition(A, U, s, V, relative_tolerance)" computes \f$A = U \operatorname{diag}(s) V^\top\f$ (Golub and Van Loan, 2013), where \f$U\f$ and \f$V\f$ have orthonormal columns and \f$s\f$ holds the \f$p\f$ singular values in decreasing order \f$s_1 \ge s_2 \ge \dots \ge s_p \ge 0\f$. Both functions return the numerical rank of \f$A\f$ in `rank`.

### Symmetric and generalized eigendecompositions

For an \f$n \times n\f$ symmetric matrix \f$A\f$, \ref laplace::dense::symmetric_eigen_decomposition "symmetric_eigen_decomposition(A, w, V)" computes \f$A = V \operatorname{diag}(w) V^\top\f$, with orthogonal \f$V\f$ (\f$V^\top V = I\f$) and eigenvalues \f$w\f$ in increasing order \f$w_1 \le w_2 \le \dots \le w_n\f$.

For symmetric \f$A\f$ and symmetric positive definite \f$B\f$ of shape \f$n \times n\f$, \ref laplace::dense::generalized_symmetric_eigen_decomposition "generalized_symmetric_eigen_decomposition(A, B, w, V)" solves \f$A V = B V \operatorname{diag}(w)\f$ with \f$V^\top B V = I\f$ and increasing eigenvalues \f$w\f$.

### Null space basis

For an \f$m \times n\f$ constraint matrix \f$C\f$, \ref laplace::dense::null_space_basis "null_space_basis(C, T, relative_tolerance)" computes an orthonormal basis \f$T\f$ satisfying \f$C T = 0\f$ and \f$T^\top T = I\f$. It reallocates \f$T\f$ to shape \f$n \times (n - r)\f$, where \f$r\f$ is the numerical rank of \f$C\f$ returned in `rank`.

### Status and option types

Every function returns a \ref laplace::dense::dense_decomposition_status "dense_decomposition_status" struct containing `success`, `rank`, and a \ref laplace::dense::dense_failure "dense_failure" code in `reason`. Triangular solves take \ref laplace::dense::dense_triangle "dense_triangle" (`lower` or `upper`) and \ref laplace::dense::dense_operation "dense_operation" (`identity` or `transpose`).

## Domain and shared conventions

All functions share the same View layout rules, triangular storage convention, status reporting, rank threshold, and threading model.

### Views and triangular storage

Matrices are rank-2 Kokkos Views of \c double with `Kokkos::LayoutLeft` in a host-accessible memory space such as `Kokkos::HostSpace`. Device-memory Views such as `Kokkos::CudaSpace` fail at compile time via static assertion. Vectors are rank-1 Kokkos Views of \c double, except `perm`, which is a rank-1 View of \c int; output Views must not have a \c const value type.

Symmetric inputs read only the lower triangle, covering \f$A\f$ in `cholesky_decomposition()`, `symmetric_eigen_decomposition()`, and `generalized_symmetric_eigen_decomposition()`, plus \f$B\f$ in the generalized eigenproblem. Strictly upper-triangular entries are unread, so non-finite values there have no effect.

### Status reporting and exceptions

Numerical outcomes appear in `dense_decomposition_status` rather than exceptions. On success, `success` is \c true, `reason` is `dense_failure::none`, and `rank` holds the numerical rank for `pivoted_qr_decomposition()`, `svd_decomposition()`, and `null_space_basis()`, or \f$n\f$ otherwise. Low numerical rank is not a failure. When `success` is \c false, outputs are unspecified and `reason` is one of:
- `dense_failure::not_positive_definite`: \f$A\f$ in Cholesky or \f$B\f$ in the generalized problem is not positive definite.
- `dense_failure::singular`: a diagonal entry of \f$T\f$ in a triangular solve is zero.
- `dense_failure::no_convergence`: an iterative SVD or eigensolver failed to converge.
- `dense_failure::non_finite_input`: a NaN or an infinity appeared among the entries read.

Invalid dimensions or incompatible View shapes throw `std::invalid_argument`.

### Rank determination and output shapes

Numerical rank \f$r\f$ counts singular values \f$s_i > \text{tol} \cdot s_1\f$ in `svd_decomposition()` and `null_space_basis()`, or diagonal entries \f$|R_{kk}| > \text{tol} \cdot |R_{11}|\f$ in `pivoted_qr_decomposition()`. When `relative_tolerance < 0` (default `-1.0`), \f$\text{tol} = \max(m, n)\,\varepsilon\f$ with \f$\varepsilon = 2^{-52}\f$, matching NumPy's `matrix_rank` (Harris and others, 2020) and MATLAB's `rank`; non-negative values override \f$\text{tol}\f$.

With \f$p = \min(m, n)\f$, output shapes choose thin or full factors. In `pivoted_qr_decomposition()`, \f$Q\f$ is \f$m \times p\f$ or \f$m \times m\f$, and \f$R\f$ is \f$p \times n\f$ or \f$m \times n\f$ to match. In `svd_decomposition()`, \f$U\f$ is \f$m \times p\f$ or \f$m \times m\f$ and \f$V\f$ is \f$n \times p\f$ or \f$n \times n\f$ independently, while \f$s\f$ has length \f$p\f$. In `null_space_basis()`, \f$T\f$ must be managed and is reallocated to \f$n \times (n - r)\f$; when \f$m = 0\f$, \f$r = 0\f$ and \f$T\f$ becomes the \f$n \times n\f$ identity.

Each call runs on one thread, so callers schedule parallel decompositions.

## Method

All routines call Eigen 3.4 (Guennebaud and others, 2010) on the host, with dedicated reductions for the generalized eigenproblem and null-space basis.

For \f$A V = B V \operatorname{diag}(w)\f$ with \f$V^\top B V = I\f$, `generalized_symmetric_eigen_decomposition()` reduces the pair using the Cholesky factor \f$L\f$ of \f$B\f$:
1. Factorize \f$B = L L^\top\f$.
2. Form \f$\tilde{A} = L^{-1} A L^{-\top}\f$.
3. Solve \f$\tilde{A} Y = Y \operatorname{diag}(w)\f$.
4. Recover \f$V = L^{-\top} Y\f$, giving \f$V^\top B V = Y^\top L^{-1} B L^{-\top} Y = Y^\top Y = I\f$.

This Cholesky reduction is not backward stable when \f$B\f$ is ill-conditioned: eigenvalues near zero incur error of order \f$\varepsilon \|A\| \|B^{-1}\|\f$ (Golub and Van Loan, 2013). For `null_space_basis()`, \f$T\f$ comes from the right singular vectors of \f$C\f$ via Jacobi SVD (Golub and Van Loan, 2013).

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for reference conventions, backend comparisons, and test bounds. Reference values were generated by `tools/reference/dense.py` with mpmath (2023) at 200 bits on 17 rounded double-precision cases: 6 symmetric (\f$n \in \{5, \dots, 60\}\f$), 3 generalized (\f$n \in \{5, 20, 40\}\f$ with condition numbers of \f$B\f$ equal to \f$10^2\f$, \f$10^6\f$, and \f$10^3\f$), and 8 rectangular (\f$1 \times 20\f$ to \f$40 \times 25\f$).

All metrics use \f$\varepsilon = 2^{-52}\f$. Symmetric eigenvalues and singular values \f$v\f$ use \f$\max_i |v_i - v_{i,\text{ref}}| / (\varepsilon \max_i |v_{i,\text{ref}}|)\f$. Generalized eigenvalues \f$w_i\f$ with \f$B\f$-normalized \f$v_i\f$ use \f$|w_i - w_{i,\text{ref}}| / (\varepsilon \kappa_i)\f$ where \f$\kappa_i = (\|A\|_F + |w_i| \|B\|_F) \|v_i\|_2^2\f$. Residuals and orthonormality errors use the Frobenius norm \f$\|\cdot\|_F\f$:
- Cholesky and triangular solve: \f$\|L L^\top - A\|_F / (\|A\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\|\operatorname{op}(T) X - B\|_F / (\|T\|_F \|X\|_F \varepsilon)\f$.
- Pivoted QR: \f$\|Q R - A P\|_F / (\|A\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\|Q^\top Q - I\|_F / \varepsilon\f$.
- SVD (with first \f$p\f$ columns \f$U_p, V_p\f$): \f$\|U_p \operatorname{diag}(s) V_p^\top - A\|_F / (\|A\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\max(\|U^\top U - I\|_F, \|V^\top V - I\|_F) / \varepsilon\f$.
- Symmetric eigenproblem: \f$\|A V - V \operatorname{diag}(w)\|_F / (\|A\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\|V^\top V - I\|_F / \varepsilon\f$.
- Generalized eigenproblem: \f$\|A V - B V \operatorname{diag}(w)\|_F / ((\|A\|_F + \|B\|_F \max_i |w_i|) \|V\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\|V^\top B V - I\|_F / (\|V\|_F^2 \|B\|_F \varepsilon)\f$.
- Null space: \f$\|C T\|_F / (\|C\|_F \varepsilon)\f$ and \f$\|T^\top T - I\|_F / \varepsilon\f$.

| Class | Quantity | gcc | CUDA | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Symmetric | Eigenvalues \f$w\f$ | 12.2 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 12.2 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 19 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Symmetric | Eigenproblem residual | 9.85 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 9.92 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 15 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Symmetric | Orthonormality | 71.0 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 72.2 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 110 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Symmetric | Cholesky residual | 0.56 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.62 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 1 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Symmetric | Triangular solve residual | 0.33 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.44 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 1 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Generalized | Eigenvalues \f$w\f$ | 1270.3 \f$\varepsilon \kappa_i\f$ | 1270.3 \f$\varepsilon \kappa_i\f$ | 1910 \f$\varepsilon \kappa_i\f$ |
| Generalized | Eigenproblem residual | 0.16 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.19 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.3 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Generalized | Orthonormality | 0.07 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.10 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 0.15 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | Singular values \f$s\f$ | 7.93 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 7.93 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 12 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | SVD residual | 14.38 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 14.39 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 22 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | SVD orthonormality | 44.17 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 44.53 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 67 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | Pivoted QR residual | 1.55 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 1.56 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 2.4 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | Pivoted QR orthonormality | 12.33 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 14.12 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 22 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | Null space residual | 0.94 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 1.74 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 2.7 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |
| Rectangular | Null space orthonormality | 12.09 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 13.07 \f$\varepsilon\f$ | 20 \f$\varepsilon\f$ |

On the generalized pair with condition of \f$B\f$ equal to \f$10^6\f$, error reaches \f$1270.3\,\varepsilon \kappa_i\f$ at two zero eigenvalues from the Cholesky reduction, while pairs with condition \f$10^2\f$ and \f$10^3\f$ stay within \f$1.4\,\varepsilon \kappa_i\f$. Across all 8 rectangular cases, SVD and pivoted QR matched the expected ranks and null-space columns matched \f$n - r\f$ whenever \f$m < n\f$; see `docs/numerics.md` for full logs.

## Execution times

Timings measured in `benchmarks/dense.cpp` on one gcc thread are in milliseconds per call across dimensions \f$K \in \{20, 50, 200, 1000\}\f$:

| \f$K\f$ | Cholesky | Triangular solve | Pivoted QR | SVD | Symmetric eigen | Generalized eigen | Null space (\f$3 \times K\f$) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 20 | 0.0008 ms | 0.0012 ms | 0.0063 ms | 0.055 ms | 0.011 ms | 0.016 ms | 0.0026 ms |
| 50 | 0.0066 ms | 0.011 ms | 0.053 ms | 0.50 ms | 0.088 ms | 0.14 ms | 0.0059 ms |
| 200 | 0.16 ms | 0.52 ms | 2.18 ms | 10.4 ms | 5.97 ms | 6.53 ms | 0.070 ms |
| 1000 | 15.6 ms | 44.3 ms | 194 ms | 608 ms | 459 ms | 736 ms | 3.3 ms |

Triangular solves used \f$K\f$ right-hand sides; pivoted QR formed \f$Q\f$, and SVD formed \f$U\f$ and \f$V\f$.

## Example

The following example computes an orthonormal null-space basis for a \f$2 \times 4\f$ constraint matrix:

```cpp
#include <laplace/dense/dense.hpp>

constexpr int m = 2;
constexpr int n = 4;
Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutLeft, Kokkos::HostSpace> C("C", m, n);

C(0, 0) = 1.0;  C(0, 1) = 1.0;  C(0, 2) = 1.0;  C(0, 3) = 1.0;
C(1, 0) = -3.0; C(1, 1) = -1.0; C(1, 2) = 1.0;  C(1, 3) = 3.0;

Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutLeft, Kokkos::HostSpace> T("T", 1, 1);
const laplace::dense::dense_decomposition_status status =
    laplace::dense::null_space_basis(C, T);

if (status.success) {
  const int null_dim = n - status.rank;  // rank is 2; T has shape 4 x 2
}
```

